# Names only. Never put real values in this file; real values go in .env (git-ignored)
# and in Vercel/Supabase environment settings.
#
# Safe for the browser (public by design; access is enforced by RLS):
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=
#
# Server only. Never prefix with NEXT_PUBLIC_ and never commit. Video upload (M4A) stays disabled until the secret key
# and all four R2 settings are set. The R2 key is scoped to the one private bucket (no public domain). The secret key is
# used only to call the upload functions after the parent's own session has been checked:
# SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY=<server-only secret key; bypasses RLS, used only by trusted server code>
# Cloudflare R2 (private bucket, no public domain). Put the real values in .env.local, never in a NEXT_PUBLIC_ name:
# R2_ACCOUNT_ID=<32-character Cloudflare account id>
# R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=<R2 API token access key id, scoped to the one bucket>
# R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=<R2 API token secret access key>
# R2_BUCKET_NAME=<bucket name, lowercase letters, digits and hyphens>
# Check them without any network call or printed value:  npm run r2:check
# Master switch for video upload (M4A.1). Server-only. ONLY the exact value "yes" turns uploads on; missing, empty or any
# other value means OFF, in the page, in authorization and in confirmation. Keep it "no" until migration 00500 is applied,
# the bucket CORS is set and a real upload test is approved:
# RIO_UPLOADS_ENABLED=no
# Master switch for FINAL SUBMISSION (M4B). Server-only. ONLY the exact value "yes" turns it on; missing, empty or any other
# value means OFF, in the page, in the server action and when its dependencies are built. Keep it "no": an equal ETag does not
# prove what a video contains, so final submission must stay off until server-side media inspection exists (release blocker B1),
# migration 00600 has been applied, and the owner has approved. It is separate from the upload switch above.
# RIO_FINALIZE_ENABLED=no
# Master switch for JUDGE PLAYBACK (M5). Server-only. ONLY the exact value "yes" lets the server sign a short-lived (5 minute) link to an
# assigned judge's entry; anything else means OFF, in the page, the action and the dependency builder. Needs the secret key and R2
# settings above and migration 00700. Keep it "no" until a real playback test is approved:
# RIO_PLAYBACK_ENABLED=no
# Confirmation e-mail after a final submission (M7). Server-only. Sending stays OFF until RIO_EMAIL_ENABLED is exactly "yes" AND the provider is configured and
# the owner has tested it. Until then the intention is only recorded in the outbox (after migration 00800), and nothing is sent. Nothing here is NEXT_PUBLIC.
# RIO_EMAIL_ENABLED=no
# EMAIL_PROVIDER=resend
# EMAIL_API_KEY=<the provider's API key, sending permission only>
# EMAIL_FROM=Rio Hospital <noreply@your-verified-domain>   (a sender on a domain verified with the provider: SPF and DKIM)
# EMAIL_REPLY_TO=<optional reply address>
# CRON_SECRET=<random string of at least 32 characters; the scheduler sends it as a Bearer token to /api/email-sweep to retry failed e-mails>
# Judge invitations from /admin/judges. Server-only. OFF unless the value is exactly "yes"; until then the form is disabled, says so, and no Supabase Auth
# call is made. Turn it on only after Supabase Authentication has working custom SMTP, the "Invite user" template from supabase/templates/invite-judge.html is
# pasted in, Site URL is set, and a test invitation has been received. Needs SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY (never NEXT_PUBLIC). The invitation e-mail is sent by Supabase
# Auth itself; this app never sends, sees or stores a password.
# RIO_STAFF_INVITES_ENABLED=no
# Manual judge accounts from /admin/judges (Add judge, Reset password). Server-only. OFF unless the value is exactly "yes"; a different switch from the invitations one, which
# it never reads. An administrator creates the account directly (no e-mail is sent): the server generates a strong password, shows it once, and the judge must choose their
# own at first sign-in. Needs SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY (never NEXT_PUBLIC). Changing it needs a redeploy.
# RIO_STAFF_PROVISIONING_ENABLED=no
#
# Hosted-database tooling (never the production project). Put these in .env.live (git-ignored):
# RIO_DEV_PROJECT_REF=               20-character ref of the DEV project; scripts/db-dev.ts refuses the .env project
# RIO_CONFIRM_PROVISION=            set to the dev ref ONLY for one `npm run staff:provision` command (needs RIO_LIVE_CONFIRM too)
# RIO_STAFF_PASSWORD=                optional, for ONE non-interactive provisioning command only; never put it in a file. The script otherwise asks at a hidden prompt
# RIO_CONFIRM_PUSH=                  set to the dev ref only when you really intend `npm run db:dev:push`
# RIO_LIVE_URL=                      dev project URL (or http://127.0.0.1:54321 for a local stack)
# RIO_LIVE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=
# RIO_LIVE_SECRET_KEY=               server-side test setup only; bypasses RLS
# RIO_LIVE_CONFIRM=                  set to the dev ref to allow `npm run test:live` to write test rows
# SUPABASE_DB_PASSWORD=              read by the Supabase CLI for the dev database
#
# IPv4 Session Pooler URL for the DEV project (dashboard > Connect > Session pooler; port 5432 only).
# Contains the database password: keep it only in .env.live and never paste it into chat or logs.
# SUPABASE_DEV_POOLER_URL=postgresql://postgres.<dev ref>:<password>@aws-N-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres
#
# Next.js app target (M3A). The app refuses to talk to any Supabase project other than the one named here,
# so a stray production .env cannot be used by accident. Put these three in .env.local (git-ignored), which
# overrides .env:
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=            the DEV project URL
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=  the DEV publishable key (public by design)
# RIO_SUPABASE_ALLOWED_REF=            the DEV project ref (20 characters), or "local" for a local stack
#
# Length of the email one-time code (M3B.2B). Server-only, optional; unset means 6, allowed 6 to 10.
# It must equal the OTP length of the Supabase project this environment talks to (Authentication, Providers, Email).
# Set it per environment (.env.local for dev, Vercel settings for production). A wrong value stops sign-in; it never guesses.
# RIO_OTP_LENGTH=6
